<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Testing for independent events using drawn numbered cards</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A single card is drawn at random from a set of <b>{@N@} cards</b> numbered 1 to {@N@}. Define the following events:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Event \(A\):</b> The card shows a multiple of <b>{@d1@}</b>.</li>
  <li><b>Event \(B\):</b> The card shows a multiple of <b>{@d2@}</b>.</li>
</ul>

<p><b>a) </b> Calculate the probability that the card is a multiple of <b>both</b> {@d1@} and {@d2@} (meaning it is a multiple of their product, {@d_int@}).</p>
<p><i>(Enter your answer as a fully simplified fraction).</i></p>
<p>\(P(A \text{ and } B) =\) [[input:ans1]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>

<p><b>b) </b> Two events are independent if the probability of their intersection equals the product of their individual probabilities. Find the exact product of the two individual probabilities.</p>
<p>\(P(A) \times P(B) =\) [[input:ans2]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>

<p><b>c) </b> Calculate the probability that the card is a multiple of {@d1@} <b>OR</b> a multiple of {@d2@}.</p>
<p>\(P =\) [[input:ans3]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>
<p><b>d) </b> Use your answers to parts a) and b) to decide: are the events \(A\) and \(B\) independent?</p>
<div>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p>First, we find the number of cards in each event by determining how many multiples fit into {@N@}:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Number of multiples of {@d1@} = \({@size_A@}\). So, \(P(A) = \frac{{@size_A@}}{{@N@}}\).</li>
  <li>Number of multiples of {@d2@} = \({@size_B@}\). So, \(P(B) = \frac{{@size_B@}}{{@N@}}\).</li>
  <li>Number of multiples of both (multiples of {@d_int@}) = \({@size_int@}\).</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Part a: Joint Probability</b><br>
The probability of drawing a card that belongs to both events is the number of shared multiples divided by the total number of cards.</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(A \text{ and } B) = \frac{{@size_int@}}{{@N@}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(A \text{ and } B) = {@ta1@} } \)</p>

<p><b>Part b: Test for Independence</b><br>
We multiply the individual probabilities of Event \(A\) and Event \(B\):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(A) \times P(B) = \frac{{@size_A@}}{{@N@}} \times \frac{{@size_B@}}{{@N@}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(A) \times P(B) = {@ta2@} } \)</p>
<p><i>Note: Because [[if test="is_indep"]]\(P(A \text{ and } B) = P(A) \times P(B)\)[[else]]\(P(A \text{ and } B) \neq P(A) \times P(B)\)[[/if]], these events are <b>{@indep_text@}</b>.</i></p>

<p><b>Part c: General Addition Rule</b><br>
Using the addition rule, we add the probabilities of \(A\) and \(B\), then subtract the intersection to avoid double-counting:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(A \text{ or } B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \text{ and } B) \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(A \text{ or } B) = \frac{{@size_A@}}{{@N@}} + \frac{{@size_B@}}{{@N@}} - \frac{{@size_int@}}{{@N@}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(A \text{ or } B) = {@ta3@} } \)</p>
<p><b>Part d: Independence</b><br>Two events are <strong>independent</strong> exactly when \(P(A \text{ and } B) = P(A) \times P(B)\). Compare parts a) and b): [[if test="is_indep"]]they are equal, so \(A\) and \(B\) <b>are</b> independent.[[else]]they are different, so \(A\) and \(B\) are <b>not</b> independent.[[/if]] Whether two "multiple of" events are independent depends on how many cards there are: it happens when the multiples fit evenly into the pack.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
